What Braess should mean in our project

We shouldn't call ordinary congestion rerouting "Braess paradox."

Classically, Braess paradox is the situation where adding network capacity/a link can make equilibrium performance worse, because flows react to congestion-dependent costs. Research also shows the phenomenon depends on traffic demand and congestion functions; it does not occur automatically every time a new edge is added.

So we'll construct an actual Braess-style topology.

Something like:

                 s2
              /      \
             /        \
Patient -- s1          s4 -- Server
             \        /
              \      /
                 s3

              + link

              s2
               |
               |
              s3

Then perform two experiments:

WITHOUT extra link
s1-s2-s4
s1-s3-s4

WITH extra link
s1-s2-s4
s1-s3-s4
s1-s2-s3-s4
...

The controller observes what happens to congestion-dependent network cost after that link becomes usable.

We need to show experimentally:

$$ C_{\text{with extra link}} > C_{\text{without extra link}} $$

under a defined demand range.

Only then do we say Braess-like/paradoxical degradation was detected.

Then our SDN controller can respond by making the problematic link unavailable to selected flows or assigning it sufficiently high cost.

That gives us:

Detect
  ↓
Quantify
  ↓
Avoid
  ↓
Measure improvement

rather than merely demonstrating the paradox.

Where healthcare becomes genuinely useful

Here's the part I think can make your project much stronger.

Don't necessarily make Braess avoidance identical for every flow.

Imagine:

NORMAL flows
████████████████

CRITICAL patient
██

A network-wide optimum and a critical patient's QoS requirement may not always be identical objectives.

So the controller could evaluate:

$$ C_{f,e} = w_D^{(k)}D_e+ w_J^{(k)}J_e+ w_L^{(k)}L_e+ w_U^{(k)}U_e $$

where \(k\) is:

NORMAL
URGENT
CRITICAL

Then add a Braess penalty/state:

$$ C'_{f,e}=C_{f,e}+B_e $$

where \(B_e\) represents the measured/estimated degradation associated with enabling that edge under the current traffic state.

Now we have something more interesting than:

Find shortest path.

It's:

Determine whether an additional network resource is currently harmful, and dynamically control its use according to network state and healthcare traffic criticality.

That's a much stronger research story.

